# Hostile audit — Target C Sections 127–129

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**Reviewed:** face fidelity/global defect injection, bounded-face train-loop exclusion,
and corridor phase purity.
**Disposition:** **PASS at proof-only scope.**
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

## 1. Section 127: vertex fidelity

Canonical Section 82 gives `I_r subset int(P_r)`. For another unloaded component
`I_s`, Section 82 gives `I_s subset int(P_s)` and Section 83 gives disjoint cell
interiors, so no vertex of `I_s` lies in `int(P_r)`.

If a loaded vertex `b` is not an attachment of cell `r`, then `b` is an extreme point
of the loaded force hull and cannot belong to the convex hull of the other loaded
vertices, which contains `P_r`. Hence no omitted loaded vertex lies in `P_r`.

Thus

\[
V(G)\cap\operatorname{int}P_r=I_r.
\]

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 2. Section 127: omitted-edge test

Let `C` be a bounded face of the induced harmonic graph `H_r`. Its open interior lies
in `int(P_r)` and contains no positive-core vertex by the preceding result.

A straight positive-contact edge not in `H_r` cannot enter and leave `C` without
meeting `partial C`. Proper intersections violate contact planarity. If both
intersections are common boundary vertices, both endpoints lie in the induced vertex
set `I_r union A_r`, so the contact is already an edge of `H_r` and cannot cut the
interior of one of its faces. All other cases force a proper crossing.

Hence every bounded local face is an actual bounded global positive-core face.
Contact-isolated source points inside the region do not invalidate this: they have no
contact edges and are not vertices of the positive-contact core.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 3. Section 127: across-cell multiplicity and defect algebra

If one global bounded face were counted in two cells, its nonempty open interior would
lie in two cell interiors, contradicting Section 83. Therefore local bounded faces
inject into global bounded faces with their degrees unchanged.

Consequently

\[
\sum_rP_{{\rm exc},r}\le4f+Q.
\]

Every nonquadrilateral bounded face has degree at least five, so `f<=Q` and
`4f+Q<=5Q`. Canonical Section 63 gives

\[
e_{BB}=Q+2k+u_B+c_B,
\qquad k=d_H+s_Z,
\]

hence `Q<=e_BB-k`. Therefore

\[
\sum_rP_{{\rm exc},r}
\le4f+Q\le5Q\le5(e_{BB}-k).
\]

The Section-122 composition subtracts an upper bound for the nonnegative exceptional
perimeter from the valid aggregate lower bound, so the inequality direction is correct.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 4. Section 128: Jordan shore contains no loaded endpoint

Assume a nontrivial train terminates twice on one bounded global face. Closing its
midpoint path through the open face gives a simple closed curve inside the convex force
hull. The bounded Jordan domain is contained in the convex hull of that curve and hence
inside the force hull.

A loaded force-hull vertex cannot lie in that open Jordan domain: it is an extreme
boundary point, whereas membership in the open domain would give it a planar
neighborhood contained in the force hull. Therefore the bounded shore contains no
active diameter endpoint and no diameter edge crosses the cut.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 5. Section 128: contact resultant and summed equilibrium

The closed curve crosses the positive-contact graph exactly in the parallel train
contacts. The train orientation is coherent across every crossed rhombus, so all cut
contact vectors have one common direction `u`. Relative-interior KKT normalization
makes every contact weight strictly positive. Thus the total entering contact
resultant is

\[
\left(\sum_{e\in E(\tau)}\alpha_e\right)u\ne0.
\]

Summing vertex equilibrium over the bounded shore cancels all internal forces. No
diameter force crosses the shore, so the contact-cut resultant must be zero, a
contradiction.

No finite shear, old response theorem, or affine-kernel assertion on isolated
coordinates is used.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 6. Section 129: exact route accounting

For any directed unit-edge route from `a` to `b` with chord direction `q`,

\[
m-|a-b|=\sum_i(1-q\cdot v_i).
\]

A boundary occurrence whose projective direction is separated from `[q]` by at least
`eta` has `q·v_i<=cos eta` regardless of whether its route orientation points forward
or back, so it costs at least `1-cos eta`.

Orient both boundary arcs from `a` to `b`. Their occurrence sets are disjoint and their
combined excess is exactly

\[
\deg C-2\operatorname{diam}C.
\]

This proves the occurrence count and weighted alignment inequalities with no repeated
charging.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 7. Section 129: Type-II and train-end composition

Canonical Section 84 gives, for a linear-degree bounded inactive face,

\[
\deg C_D-2\operatorname{diam}C_D=o(D).
\]

For every fixed angular scale the finite denominator `1-cos eta` is positive, hence
only `o(D)` boundary occurrences can be transverse to the corridor direction.

Section 127 makes local harmonic defect faces actual global faces while preserving
physical boundary occurrences. Train endpoints are a subset of those occurrences, so
the same phase-purity bound applies without assuming every boundary edge is a train
endpoint. Section 128 ensures two endpoints on one bounded face come from distinct
trains.

The host interpretation adds no false free support: the same corridor route excess pays
the physical alignment defect of the endpoint cohort. The theorem explicitly stops
short of claiming that corridor phase purity alone excludes an oblique lattice patch.

**Verdict:** PASS.

## 8. Scope and final disposition

- Section 127 is finite on the post-ZF positive-contact core; its Section-84 consequence
  is Type II.
- Section 128 is finite and exact in the normalized positive core.
- Section 129 is Type II for the `o(D)` phase-purity conclusion.
- Contact-isolated full-source points are retained throughout.
- No full-source extension of the Section-57 affine kernel is used.
- None of the three theorems supplies the missing aggregate outer-detour upper bound or
  a strict diameter-decreasing source competitor.

**Final verdict:** Sections 127–129 survive hostile audit at proof-only scope. They
safely absorb the unique mathematical content of PR #351 into the post-123 full-core
structural branch. C-II remains open.